          "text": "A CUDA graph replay is faster than eager kernel launches primarily because it eliminates the CPU-side overhead associated with the CUDA driver and runtime. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CPU must perform a significant amount of work for every single kernel: it validates parameters, allocates resources, and constructs a command buffer to send to the GPU. This process involves context switching and synchronization between the user space and the kernel space of the operating system. By contrast, a CUDA graph captures this entire sequence of operations\u2014including kernel launches, memory copies, and synchronization events\u2014into a single, immutable structure. When the graph is replayed, the CPU submits a single command to the GPU to execute the pre-compiled sequence. This drastically reduces the CPU-to-GPU communication latency and allows the GPU to begin executing the first kernel almost immediately, rather than waiting for the CPU to catch up with a stream of individual launch commands.\n\nThis speedup is most noticeable in scenarios where the kernels themselves are very short (low arithmetic intensity) or when the workload involves a massive number of small kernels. In these cases, the \"launch latency\" (the time it takes for the CPU to tell the GPU to start) often exceeds the actual execution time of the kernels. By batching these launches into a graph, the GPU can",
